    ($SATQ$ea8f4658$SATQ$, $SATQ$5f6aff36-fba7-4d47-9254-29101c8c3de5$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$d7a09fbc-b19b-42e6-9c59-1316daf467be$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>When particles are suspended in liquid (like pollen in a water glass), they will zigzag randomly through the liquid and collide with one another in perpetuity. This type of random, continuous <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> is known as Brownian motion, can be observed throughout the natural world.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"55d9445c-c09d-40ad-aca2-601a75031a65","content":"<p>movement: which</p>"},{"id":"266dd10f-77c3-4a92-8908-0edcdfd99bc3","content":"<p>movement, which</p>"},{"id":"f38db23e-6e72-41cf-9c63-6cf49f3f4c85","content":"<p>movement which</p>"},{"id":"54fc3443-95a7-4280-a182-cb6264c904de","content":"<p>movement. Which</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["266dd10f-77c3-4a92-8908-0edcdfd99bc3"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. This choice correctly uses commas to set off the nonessential relative clause "which is known as Brownian motion" that provides extra information about the "random, continuous movement" that isn&rsquo;t necessary for the function of the sentence.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice results in a punctuation error. The relative clause "which is known as Brownian motion" is a nonessential supplement. Nonessential supplements need to be set apart from the rest of the sentence with a pair of commas, dashes, or parentheses, so we can&rsquo;t use a colon here. Also, notice that colons can only come after an independent clause, which isn&rsquo;t the case here. Choice C is incorrect. This choice results in a punctuation error. The relative clause "which is known as Brownian motion" is a nonessential supplement, so it should be separated from the rest of the sentence by a pair of matching punctuation marks. We already have a comma after "motion," so we need to add a comma before "which." This choice is missing that comma. Choice D is incorrect. This choice results in a sentence fragment. "This type of random, continuous movement" is not an independent clause and can&rsquo;t stand alone as a full sentence, so we can&rsquo;t put a period here.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-BOU-E010$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$c619320b-3d0c-491f-94bb-e062ea5b2348$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"ea8f4658","external_id":"5f6aff36-fba7-4d47-9254-29101c8c3de5","vaultid":"d7a09fbc-b19b-42e6-9c59-1316daf467be","uId":"7cdcfaa6-bf0d-4458-bc20-5348b67839ab","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>When particles are suspended in liquid (like pollen in a water glass), they will zigzag randomly through the liquid and collide with one another in perpetuity. This type of random, continuous <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> is known as Brownian motion, can be observed throughout the natural world.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"55d9445c-c09d-40ad-aca2-601a75031a65","content":"<p>movement: which</p>"},{"id":"266dd10f-77c3-4a92-8908-0edcdfd99bc3","content":"<p>movement, which</p>"},{"id":"f38db23e-6e72-41cf-9c63-6cf49f3f4c85","content":"<p>movement which</p>"},{"id":"54fc3443-95a7-4280-a182-cb6264c904de","content":"<p>movement. Which</p>"}],"keys":["266dd10f-77c3-4a92-8908-0edcdfd99bc3"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. This choice correctly uses commas to set off the nonessential relative clause \"which is known as Brownian motion\" that provides extra information about the \"random, continuous movement\" that isn&rsquo;t necessary for the function of the sentence.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice results in a punctuation error. The relative clause \"which is known as Brownian motion\" is a nonessential supplement. Nonessential supplements need to be set apart from the rest of the sentence with a pair of commas, dashes, or parentheses, so we can&rsquo;t use a colon here. Also, notice that colons can only come after an independent clause, which isn&rsquo;t the case here. Choice C is incorrect. This choice results in a punctuation error. The relative clause \"which is known as Brownian motion\" is a nonessential supplement, so it should be separated from the rest of the sentence by a pair of matching punctuation marks. We already have a comma after \"motion,\" so we need to add a comma before \"which.\" This choice is missing that comma. Choice D is incorrect. This choice results in a sentence fragment. \"This type of random, continuous movement\" is not an independent clause and can&rsquo;t stand alone as a full sentence, so we can&rsquo;t put a period here.</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-BOU-E010","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"c619320b-3d0c-491f-94bb-e062ea5b2348","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959358,"updateDate":1691007959358}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959358, 1691007959358),
    ($SATQ$eb03096e$SATQ$, $SATQ$c99ab588-0747-4b82-ae2e-d8a46f360a23$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>According to the University of Hawaiʻi at Mānoa, <span lang="haw">aloha ʻāina</span> is &ldquo;a recognition, commitment, and practice sustaining the ea&mdash;or life breath&rdquo; between the Hawaiian people and their natural environments. The concept has been proudly embodied <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> Native Hawaiians for generations, contributing to the lush flora and renowned beauty of the islands.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"68677fd4-5e96-442e-a6e3-c9049fff54bc","content":"<p>by;</p>"},{"id":"9f4f6a3f-8033-4ee5-9849-47eea7afafc0","content":"<p>by:</p>"},{"id":"b13844fa-1aff-43c1-bc5c-b1dd98fe6cae","content":"<p>by,</p>"},{"id":"453ee52a-828e-4255-8984-45251043f8dd","content":"<p>by</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["453ee52a-828e-4255-8984-45251043f8dd"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation use between a preposition and its complement. No punctuation is needed between the preposition "by" and its complement "Native Hawaiians." The complement completes the meaning of the preposition in the phrase "proudly embodied by Native Hawaiians," and any punctuation within it results in an ungrammatical sentence.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the preposition and its complement. Choice B is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the preposition and its complement. Choice C is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the preposition and its complement.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"eb03096e","external_id":"c99ab588-0747-4b82-ae2e-d8a46f360a23","vaultid":null,"uId":"e41705b2-a6e2-41e9-81c8-50bcbfd76e05","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>According to the University of Hawaiʻi at Mānoa, <span lang=\"haw\">aloha ʻāina</span> is &ldquo;a recognition, commitment, and practice sustaining the ea&mdash;or life breath&rdquo; between the Hawaiian people and their natural environments. The concept has been proudly embodied <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> Native Hawaiians for generations, contributing to the lush flora and renowned beauty of the islands.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"68677fd4-5e96-442e-a6e3-c9049fff54bc","content":"<p>by;</p>"},{"id":"9f4f6a3f-8033-4ee5-9849-47eea7afafc0","content":"<p>by:</p>"},{"id":"b13844fa-1aff-43c1-bc5c-b1dd98fe6cae","content":"<p>by,</p>"},{"id":"453ee52a-828e-4255-8984-45251043f8dd","content":"<p>by</p>"}],"keys":["453ee52a-828e-4255-8984-45251043f8dd"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation use between a preposition and its complement. No punctuation is needed between the preposition \"by\" and its complement \"Native Hawaiians.\" The complement completes the meaning of the preposition in the phrase \"proudly embodied by Native Hawaiians,\" and any punctuation within it results in an ungrammatical sentence.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the preposition and its complement. Choice B is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the preposition and its complement. Choice C is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the preposition and its complement.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323050,"updateDate":1730147323050}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323050, 1730147323050),
    ($SATQ$eb95235b$SATQ$, $SATQ$b13612d3-41b3-4be6-9c7c-3b2f739b4cdb$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The Lim&oacute;n technique, developed by Mexican-born dancer and choreographer Jose Lim&oacute;n, is known for its emphasis on breath control and its interplay of weight and <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> dancers may explore, for example, the moment of mid-air suspension at the top of a jump.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"ba1286f3-cc4a-49f3-bc24-70a0f1bbe313","content":"<p>weightlessness</p>"},{"id":"25140d56-f301-401e-bd38-3255fb099813","content":"<p>weightlessness which</p>"},{"id":"a2159a5e-b392-426e-8fe2-7e0ea94e9d6b","content":"<p>weightlessness,</p>"},{"id":"424b4d15-5533-4c8c-9cfb-423fb4f170dc","content":"<p>weightlessness;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["424b4d15-5533-4c8c-9cfb-423fb4f170dc"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation within a sentence. This choice uses a semicolon in a conventional way to join the first main clause ("The Lim&oacute;n&hellip;weightlessness") and the second main clause ("dancers&hellip;jump").</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The two main clauses are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction. Choice B is incorrect. The relative pronoun "which" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Without a conjunction following it, a comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"eb95235b","external_id":"b13612d3-41b3-4be6-9c7c-3b2f739b4cdb","vaultid":null,"uId":"0833b94d-470c-4b8f-93bb-cfe60528bf62","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The Lim&oacute;n technique, developed by Mexican-born dancer and choreographer Jose Lim&oacute;n, is known for its emphasis on breath control and its interplay of weight and <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> dancers may explore, for example, the moment of mid-air suspension at the top of a jump.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"ba1286f3-cc4a-49f3-bc24-70a0f1bbe313","content":"<p>weightlessness</p>"},{"id":"25140d56-f301-401e-bd38-3255fb099813","content":"<p>weightlessness which</p>"},{"id":"a2159a5e-b392-426e-8fe2-7e0ea94e9d6b","content":"<p>weightlessness,</p>"},{"id":"424b4d15-5533-4c8c-9cfb-423fb4f170dc","content":"<p>weightlessness;</p>"}],"keys":["424b4d15-5533-4c8c-9cfb-423fb4f170dc"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation within a sentence. This choice uses a semicolon in a conventional way to join the first main clause (\"The Lim&oacute;n&hellip;weightlessness\") and the second main clause (\"dancers&hellip;jump\").</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The two main clauses are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction. Choice B is incorrect. The relative pronoun \"which\" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Without a conjunction following it, a comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323050,"updateDate":1730147323050}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323050, 1730147323050),
    ($SATQ$ec08463d$SATQ$, $SATQ$16b9a9a2-b5d1-4f24-8427-a82d6389baaa$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$c635e45b-16d7-4c6a-9b99-05154f7c718f$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Botanists recognize over fifty different species of sunflower. One species, the silverleaf sunflower, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> both an early-flowering ecotype that tends to grow in coastal areas and a late-flowering ecotype that grows inland.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"69701cc8-50e5-4203-9679-4d9163463454","content":"<p>having included</p>"},{"id":"58ee262b-ca26-4d5e-b111-5b7a19f93bb4","content":"<p>including</p>"},{"id":"af2fc7d8-7ca0-41b7-a70b-33bc98d3a675","content":"<p>to include</p>"},{"id":"9380e61a-6693-42b9-856c-3b0bf0832da0","content":"<p>includes</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["9380e61a-6693-42b9-856c-3b0bf0832da0"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. This verb needs to function as the main verb in the sentence, with the subject &ldquo;one species,&rdquo; so it needs to be conjugated. This choice gives us the singular present tense &ldquo;includes,&rdquo; which is the only conjugated form of the verb among the choices.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This doesn&rsquo;t complete the text in a way that conforms to the conventions of Standard English. This verb needs to function as the main verb in the sentence, with the subject &ldquo;one species,&rdquo; so it needs to be conjugated. &ldquo;Having included&rdquo; is a participle form of the verb: it&rsquo;s not conjugated and doesn&rsquo;t function like a normal verb.&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect. This doesn&rsquo;t complete the text in a way that conforms to the conventions of Standard English. This verb needs to function as the main verb in the sentence, with the subject &ldquo;one species,&rdquo; so it needs to be conjugated. &ldquo;Including&rdquo; is the gerund form of the verb: it&rsquo;s not conjugated and doesn&rsquo;t function like a normal verb.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect. This doesn&rsquo;t complete the text in a way that conforms to the conventions of Standard English. This verb needs to function as the main verb in the sentence, with the subject &ldquo;one species,&rdquo; so it needs to be conjugated. &ldquo;To include&rdquo; is the infinitive form of the verb: it&rsquo;s not conjugated.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-655-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$8a97127c-f3a0-4fa5-928b-f7cd3dcae2f5$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"ec08463d","external_id":"16b9a9a2-b5d1-4f24-8427-a82d6389baaa","vaultid":"c635e45b-16d7-4c6a-9b99-05154f7c718f","uId":"e1d9fb53-cbc3-4aca-af53-4d07da5fe5f2","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Botanists recognize over fifty different species of sunflower. One species, the silverleaf sunflower, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> both an early-flowering ecotype that tends to grow in coastal areas and a late-flowering ecotype that grows inland.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"69701cc8-50e5-4203-9679-4d9163463454","content":"<p>having included</p>"},{"id":"58ee262b-ca26-4d5e-b111-5b7a19f93bb4","content":"<p>including</p>"},{"id":"af2fc7d8-7ca0-41b7-a70b-33bc98d3a675","content":"<p>to include</p>"},{"id":"9380e61a-6693-42b9-856c-3b0bf0832da0","content":"<p>includes</p>"}],"keys":["9380e61a-6693-42b9-856c-3b0bf0832da0"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. This verb needs to function as the main verb in the sentence, with the subject &ldquo;one species,&rdquo; so it needs to be conjugated. This choice gives us the singular present tense &ldquo;includes,&rdquo; which is the only conjugated form of the verb among the choices.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This doesn&rsquo;t complete the text in a way that conforms to the conventions of Standard English. This verb needs to function as the main verb in the sentence, with the subject &ldquo;one species,&rdquo; so it needs to be conjugated. &ldquo;Having included&rdquo; is a participle form of the verb: it&rsquo;s not conjugated and doesn&rsquo;t function like a normal verb.&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect. This doesn&rsquo;t complete the text in a way that conforms to the conventions of Standard English. This verb needs to function as the main verb in the sentence, with the subject &ldquo;one species,&rdquo; so it needs to be conjugated. &ldquo;Including&rdquo; is the gerund form of the verb: it&rsquo;s not conjugated and doesn&rsquo;t function like a normal verb.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect. This doesn&rsquo;t complete the text in a way that conforms to the conventions of Standard English. This verb needs to function as the main verb in the sentence, with the subject &ldquo;one species,&rdquo; so it needs to be conjugated. &ldquo;To include&rdquo; is the infinitive form of the verb: it&rsquo;s not conjugated.&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-655-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"8a97127c-f3a0-4fa5-928b-f7cd3dcae2f5","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959341,"updateDate":1691007959341}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959341, 1691007959341),
    ($SATQ$ecba68b5$SATQ$, $SATQ$2fea820d-294a-42a9-ae07-e544fc69cdb9$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Mathematician Grigori Perelman, sometimes in conjunction with mathematicians Richard <span data-ssml-sub-alias="S">S.</span> Hamilton and Shing-Tung Yau, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> credited with proving the Poincar&eacute; conjecture. Having built on Hamilton&rsquo;s previous work to solve the proof, Perelman has insisted that Hamilton receive credit. Yau later found and closed gaps in Perelman&rsquo;s proof, persuading some mathematicians that he deserves credit as well.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"6c33e639-39ec-43cc-8f65-992657d6eddd","content":"<p>are</p>"},{"id":"76ca71ca-a4bd-479f-acfd-c8956703ae5f","content":"<p>have been</p>"},{"id":"8563bffa-d8e5-4093-9079-ea215fa50a86","content":"<p>are being</p>"},{"id":"c379eb94-7c2c-4fb7-a9b8-7bfb45e4fbe6","content":"<p>is</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["c379eb94-7c2c-4fb7-a9b8-7bfb45e4fbe6"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-verb agreement. The singular verb "is credited" agrees in number with the singular subject "mathematician Grigori Perelman."</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the plural verb "are credited" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject "mathematician Grigori Perelman." Choice B is incorrect because the plural verb "have been credited" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject "mathematician Grigori Perelman." Choice C is incorrect because the plural verb "are being credited" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject "mathematician Grigori Perelman."</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"ecba68b5","external_id":"2fea820d-294a-42a9-ae07-e544fc69cdb9","vaultid":null,"uId":"85b7d49a-6545-47b9-bdf2-2d297525c2ba","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Mathematician Grigori Perelman, sometimes in conjunction with mathematicians Richard <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"S\">S.</span> Hamilton and Shing-Tung Yau, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> credited with proving the Poincar&eacute; conjecture. Having built on Hamilton&rsquo;s previous work to solve the proof, Perelman has insisted that Hamilton receive credit. Yau later found and closed gaps in Perelman&rsquo;s proof, persuading some mathematicians that he deserves credit as well.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"6c33e639-39ec-43cc-8f65-992657d6eddd","content":"<p>are</p>"},{"id":"76ca71ca-a4bd-479f-acfd-c8956703ae5f","content":"<p>have been</p>"},{"id":"8563bffa-d8e5-4093-9079-ea215fa50a86","content":"<p>are being</p>"},{"id":"c379eb94-7c2c-4fb7-a9b8-7bfb45e4fbe6","content":"<p>is</p>"}],"keys":["c379eb94-7c2c-4fb7-a9b8-7bfb45e4fbe6"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-verb agreement. The singular verb \"is credited\" agrees in number with the singular subject \"mathematician Grigori Perelman.\"</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the plural verb \"are credited\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject \"mathematician Grigori Perelman.\" Choice B is incorrect because the plural verb \"have been credited\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject \"mathematician Grigori Perelman.\" Choice C is incorrect because the plural verb \"are being credited\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject \"mathematician Grigori Perelman.\"</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669205,"updateDate":1743430669205}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669205, 1743430669205),
    ($SATQ$eeb14722$SATQ$, $SATQ$797b02ff-4b49-42f5-bec2-3b20e73db91a$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The soundtrack to Mira Nair&rsquo;s 1991 film <em>Mississippi Masala</em> expressively captures the clashing of cultures that happens when <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> (a young Indian woman from Uganda and a young African American man from Mississippi) meet. Featured throughout the film are songs from Uganda&rsquo;s Afrigo Band, the Indian composer L. Subramaniam, and the Mississippi blues musician Sam Chatmon.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"4d12d082-142a-4c22-ae24-3994d69a8086","content":"<p>it&rsquo;s two protagonists</p>"},{"id":"2ffc95b3-77aa-4533-acf4-3f758e1ec26b","content":"<p>its two protagonist&rsquo;s</p>"},{"id":"75cf7b5d-5f52-4ce8-92d2-e77e1c73751f","content":"<p>it&rsquo;s two protagonist&rsquo;s</p>"},{"id":"4564dbab-e688-494b-aed3-cfbcda0e10b8","content":"<p>its two protagonists</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["4564dbab-e688-494b-aed3-cfbcda0e10b8"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The conventions being tested are the use of possessive determiners and the use of plural nouns. The singular possessive determiner "its"&mdash;which agrees in number with the singular noun phrase "Mira Nair&rsquo;s 1991 film <em>Mississippi Masala</em>"&mdash;and the plural noun "protagonists" correctly indicate that Nair&rsquo;s film has multiple protagonists.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because "it&rsquo;s" is the contraction for "it is," not a possessive determiner. Choice B is incorrect because the context requires the plural noun "protagonists," not the singular possessive noun "protagonist&rsquo;s." Choice C is incorrect because the context requires the possessive determiner "its" and the plural noun "protagonists," not the contraction "it&rsquo;s" or the singular possessive noun "protagonist&rsquo;s."</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"eeb14722","external_id":"797b02ff-4b49-42f5-bec2-3b20e73db91a","vaultid":null,"uId":"33899403-e470-49e3-bbd2-7deaa63b3d33","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The soundtrack to Mira Nair&rsquo;s 1991 film <em>Mississippi Masala</em> expressively captures the clashing of cultures that happens when <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> (a young Indian woman from Uganda and a young African American man from Mississippi) meet. Featured throughout the film are songs from Uganda&rsquo;s Afrigo Band, the Indian composer L. Subramaniam, and the Mississippi blues musician Sam Chatmon.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"4d12d082-142a-4c22-ae24-3994d69a8086","content":"<p>it&rsquo;s two protagonists</p>"},{"id":"2ffc95b3-77aa-4533-acf4-3f758e1ec26b","content":"<p>its two protagonist&rsquo;s</p>"},{"id":"75cf7b5d-5f52-4ce8-92d2-e77e1c73751f","content":"<p>it&rsquo;s two protagonist&rsquo;s</p>"},{"id":"4564dbab-e688-494b-aed3-cfbcda0e10b8","content":"<p>its two protagonists</p>"}],"keys":["4564dbab-e688-494b-aed3-cfbcda0e10b8"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The conventions being tested are the use of possessive determiners and the use of plural nouns. The singular possessive determiner \"its\"&mdash;which agrees in number with the singular noun phrase \"Mira Nair&rsquo;s 1991 film <em>Mississippi Masala</em>\"&mdash;and the plural noun \"protagonists\" correctly indicate that Nair&rsquo;s film has multiple protagonists.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because \"it&rsquo;s\" is the contraction for \"it is,\" not a possessive determiner. Choice B is incorrect because the context requires the plural noun \"protagonists,\" not the singular possessive noun \"protagonist&rsquo;s.\" Choice C is incorrect because the context requires the possessive determiner \"its\" and the plural noun \"protagonists,\" not the contraction \"it&rsquo;s\" or the singular possessive noun \"protagonist&rsquo;s.\"</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323049,"updateDate":1730147323049}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323049, 1730147323049),
    ($SATQ$eee7536f$SATQ$, $SATQ$97234b6e-84b2-4fa6-8f23-d52ad8303c8e$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>On March 27, 2004, after nine years as a professional boxer, Isra Girgrah retired. The Yemeni American athlete <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> her career with a record of twenty-eight wins, three losses, and two ties.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"c928bb88-0f07-4861-8265-587302092327","content":"<p>finish</p>"},{"id":"6c69fc61-d4bf-4e5f-9475-0e4fdd9aa27a","content":"<p>has been finishing</p>"},{"id":"ea40e679-45af-4e2b-ab7b-edd4f35e706d","content":"<p>finished</p>"},{"id":"511a4a34-6196-4a8b-97c0-01b85136c313","content":"<p>will be finishing</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["ea40e679-45af-4e2b-ab7b-edd4f35e706d"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of a verb to express tense in a sentence. In this choice, the past tense verb &ldquo;finished&rdquo; is consistent with the other past tense verb (&ldquo;retired&rdquo;) used to refer to Girgrah&rsquo;s retirement in 2004.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the present tense verb &ldquo;finish&rdquo; isn&rsquo;t consistent with the other past tense verb (&ldquo;retired&rdquo;) used to refer to Girgrah&rsquo;s retirement in 2004. Furthermore, the plural verb &ldquo;finish&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the sentence&rsquo;s singular subject &ldquo;The Yemeni American athlete.&rdquo; Choice B is incorrect because the present perfect progressive tense verb &ldquo;has been finishing&rdquo; isn&rsquo;t consistent with the other past tense verb (&ldquo;retired&rdquo;) used to refer to Girgrah&rsquo;s retirement in 2004. Choice D is incorrect because the future progressive tense verb &ldquo;will be finishing&rdquo; isn&rsquo;t consistent with the other past tense verb (&ldquo;retired&rdquo;) used to refer to Girgrah&rsquo;s retirement in 2004.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"eee7536f","external_id":"97234b6e-84b2-4fa6-8f23-d52ad8303c8e","vaultid":null,"uId":"8838afe7-753d-4bcb-a5d9-f7c9ce5065f7","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>On March 27, 2004, after nine years as a professional boxer, Isra Girgrah retired. The Yemeni American athlete <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> her career with a record of twenty-eight wins, three losses, and two ties.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"c928bb88-0f07-4861-8265-587302092327","content":"<p>finish</p>"},{"id":"6c69fc61-d4bf-4e5f-9475-0e4fdd9aa27a","content":"<p>has been finishing</p>"},{"id":"ea40e679-45af-4e2b-ab7b-edd4f35e706d","content":"<p>finished</p>"},{"id":"511a4a34-6196-4a8b-97c0-01b85136c313","content":"<p>will be finishing</p>"}],"keys":["ea40e679-45af-4e2b-ab7b-edd4f35e706d"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of a verb to express tense in a sentence. In this choice, the past tense verb &ldquo;finished&rdquo; is consistent with the other past tense verb (&ldquo;retired&rdquo;) used to refer to Girgrah&rsquo;s retirement in 2004.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the present tense verb &ldquo;finish&rdquo; isn&rsquo;t consistent with the other past tense verb (&ldquo;retired&rdquo;) used to refer to Girgrah&rsquo;s retirement in 2004. Furthermore, the plural verb &ldquo;finish&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the sentence&rsquo;s singular subject &ldquo;The Yemeni American athlete.&rdquo; Choice B is incorrect because the present perfect progressive tense verb &ldquo;has been finishing&rdquo; isn&rsquo;t consistent with the other past tense verb (&ldquo;retired&rdquo;) used to refer to Girgrah&rsquo;s retirement in 2004. Choice D is incorrect because the future progressive tense verb &ldquo;will be finishing&rdquo; isn&rsquo;t consistent with the other past tense verb (&ldquo;retired&rdquo;) used to refer to Girgrah&rsquo;s retirement in 2004.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771169,"updateDate":1776362771169}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771169, 1776362771169),
    ($SATQ$eef91a50$SATQ$, $SATQ$27e36990-ad17-4dc7-9600-2b97b847f0c5$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$b473b4f0-2bd7-4013-8ab1-16771fc2de09$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Nine months before Rosa Parks made history by refusing to comply with the segregated seating policy on a Montgomery, Alabama, bus, a fifteen-year-old Montgomery girl named Claudette Colvin was arrested for the same <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> to some historians, Colvin&rsquo;s arrest led to Parks&rsquo;s action and eventually to the desegregation of Montgomery&rsquo;s bus system.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"8e9f8400-085f-46c8-86ef-239c04132779","content":"<p>offense. According</p>"},{"id":"44e77036-fcc2-4e27-9a03-0c3612663df0","content":"<p>offense, according</p>"},{"id":"5613f439-485e-4143-8f0b-9988ed0d601e","content":"<p>offense according</p>"},{"id":"2b22c71c-fdc4-4205-8e13-450423377cb2","content":"<p>offense and according</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["8e9f8400-085f-46c8-86ef-239c04132779"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. &ldquo;Nine months&hellip;offense&rdquo; and &ldquo;according to&hellip;system&rdquo; are both independent clauses. Separating them with a period and turning them into their own sentences is the only grammatically correct choice among the provided options. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This choice results in a comma splice error, which is a punctuation error that occurs when two independent clauses are joined by only a comma. &ldquo;Nine months&hellip;offense&rdquo; and &ldquo;according to&hellip;system&rdquo; are both independent clauses, so they need to be either joined by a semicolon, joined by a comma and a coordinating conjunction, or separated by a period. Choice C is incorrect. This choice results in a run-on sentence, which occurs when two independent clauses are joined without punctuation. &ldquo;Nine months&hellip;offense&rdquo; and &ldquo;according to&hellip;system&rdquo; are both independent clauses, so they need to be either joined by a semicolon, joined by a comma and a coordinating conjunction, or separated by a period. Choice D is incorrect. This choice results in a run-on sentence, which occurs when two independent clauses are joined without punctuation. &ldquo;Nine months&hellip;offense&rdquo; and &ldquo;according to&hellip;system&rdquo; are independent clauses, so we would need to put a comma before the coordinating conjunction &ldquo;and&rdquo; to join them properly. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-BOU-M007$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$1a4dabce-3812-497a-9a19-2269921d9570$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"eef91a50","external_id":"27e36990-ad17-4dc7-9600-2b97b847f0c5","vaultid":"b473b4f0-2bd7-4013-8ab1-16771fc2de09","uId":"6ac7c07b-50ab-4936-92a8-b6ded1e5acdb","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Nine months before Rosa Parks made history by refusing to comply with the segregated seating policy on a Montgomery, Alabama, bus, a fifteen-year-old Montgomery girl named Claudette Colvin was arrested for the same <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> to some historians, Colvin&rsquo;s arrest led to Parks&rsquo;s action and eventually to the desegregation of Montgomery&rsquo;s bus system.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"8e9f8400-085f-46c