    ($SATQ$f339ee39$SATQ$, $SATQ$51e3afc5-5b0e-44ac-ba31-71b85c36fa37$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Multiple newspapers <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> the Spanish-speaking population of Washington, DC, including <em>El Tiempo Latino</em> and <em>Washington Hispanic</em>.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"4b6a71bb-7d5f-42be-9c1c-f4155adb07ee","content":"<p>serve</p>"},{"id":"2ba5e278-837d-4b0a-88bb-759c9934c14a","content":"<p>having served</p>"},{"id":"f7bf698b-7218-4bb7-99ac-6e7dc106c8a5","content":"<p>to serve</p>"},{"id":"69513e5e-f4d7-4340-bc66-040f0261b5af","content":"<p>serving</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["4b6a71bb-7d5f-42be-9c1c-f4155adb07ee"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of verb forms within a sentence. A main clause requires a finite (tensed) verb to perform the action of the subject (in this case, "multiple newspapers"), and this choice supplies the finite present tense verb "serve" to indicate that there are multiple newspapers serving the Spanish-speaking population of Washington, DC.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The nonfinite participle "having served" doesn&rsquo;t supply the main clause with a finite verb. Choice C is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The nonfinite to-infinitive "to serve" doesn&rsquo;t supply the main clause with a finite verb. Choice D is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The nonfinite participle "serving" doesn&rsquo;t supply the main clause with a finite verb.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"f339ee39","external_id":"51e3afc5-5b0e-44ac-ba31-71b85c36fa37","vaultid":null,"uId":"7c46418c-4e82-473c-80b0-7d017c9f0b20","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Multiple newspapers <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> the Spanish-speaking population of Washington, DC, including <em>El Tiempo Latino</em> and <em>Washington Hispanic</em>.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"4b6a71bb-7d5f-42be-9c1c-f4155adb07ee","content":"<p>serve</p>"},{"id":"2ba5e278-837d-4b0a-88bb-759c9934c14a","content":"<p>having served</p>"},{"id":"f7bf698b-7218-4bb7-99ac-6e7dc106c8a5","content":"<p>to serve</p>"},{"id":"69513e5e-f4d7-4340-bc66-040f0261b5af","content":"<p>serving</p>"}],"keys":["4b6a71bb-7d5f-42be-9c1c-f4155adb07ee"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of verb forms within a sentence. A main clause requires a finite (tensed) verb to perform the action of the subject (in this case, \"multiple newspapers\"), and this choice supplies the finite present tense verb \"serve\" to indicate that there are multiple newspapers serving the Spanish-speaking population of Washington, DC.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The nonfinite participle \"having served\" doesn&rsquo;t supply the main clause with a finite verb. Choice C is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The nonfinite to-infinitive \"to serve\" doesn&rsquo;t supply the main clause with a finite verb. Choice D is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The nonfinite participle \"serving\" doesn&rsquo;t supply the main clause with a finite verb.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323051,"updateDate":1730147323051}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323051, 1730147323051),
    ($SATQ$f40b447c$SATQ$, $SATQ$d6592b88-b156-4a17-ab55-25262d62d208$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Featuring jagged peaks of black ink surrounded by hazy swirls of blue and green paint, Zhang Daqian&rsquo;s 1983 painting <em>Panorama of Mount Lu</em> is inspired by the tradition of <span lang="zh"><em>qingl&uuml; shanshui</em></span>, a type of Chinese landscape painting <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> by the use of blue and green hues to depict ethereal, otherworldly landscapes.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"272e642f-3f32-446e-a0ff-9594a205c9be","content":"<p>has been characterized</p>"},{"id":"0f60a1d1-2b37-409f-a2d5-1aa6baba7e38","content":"<p>will be characterized</p>"},{"id":"86e445d2-76cd-4955-b727-ff42076491b0","content":"<p>characterized</p>"},{"id":"979fe6c3-c973-4b81-9af9-87c2962fd616","content":"<p>is characterized</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["86e445d2-76cd-4955-b727-ff42076491b0"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of verb forms within a sentence. The nonfinite past participle "characterized" is correctly used within a supplementary element that modifies the main clause "Zhang...<em>shanshui</em>," defining <em>qingl&uuml; shanshui</em> and explaining some of its identifying traits.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Using the finite present perfect tense verb "has been characterized" creates a second main clause in the sentence, and the two main clauses can&rsquo;t be joined in this way by only the comma before "a type." Choice B is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Using the finite future tense verb "will be characterized" creates a second main clause in the sentence, and the two main clauses can&rsquo;t be joined in this way by only the comma before "a type." Choice D is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Using the finite present tense verb "is characterized" creates a second main clause in the sentence, and the two main clauses can&rsquo;t be joined in this way by only the comma before "a type."</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"f40b447c","external_id":"d6592b88-b156-4a17-ab55-25262d62d208","vaultid":null,"uId":"b313a844-694c-4381-8cc5-914a5814ef02","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Featuring jagged peaks of black ink surrounded by hazy swirls of blue and green paint, Zhang Daqian&rsquo;s 1983 painting <em>Panorama of Mount Lu</em> is inspired by the tradition of <span lang=\"zh\"><em>qingl&uuml; shanshui</em></span>, a type of Chinese landscape painting <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> by the use of blue and green hues to depict ethereal, otherworldly landscapes.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"272e642f-3f32-446e-a0ff-9594a205c9be","content":"<p>has been characterized</p>"},{"id":"0f60a1d1-2b37-409f-a2d5-1aa6baba7e38","content":"<p>will be characterized</p>"},{"id":"86e445d2-76cd-4955-b727-ff42076491b0","content":"<p>characterized</p>"},{"id":"979fe6c3-c973-4b81-9af9-87c2962fd616","content":"<p>is characterized</p>"}],"keys":["86e445d2-76cd-4955-b727-ff42076491b0"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of verb forms within a sentence. The nonfinite past participle \"characterized\" is correctly used within a supplementary element that modifies the main clause \"Zhang...<em>shanshui</em>,\" defining <em>qingl&uuml; shanshui</em> and explaining some of its identifying traits.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Using the finite present perfect tense verb \"has been characterized\" creates a second main clause in the sentence, and the two main clauses can&rsquo;t be joined in this way by only the comma before \"a type.\" Choice B is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Using the finite future tense verb \"will be characterized\" creates a second main clause in the sentence, and the two main clauses can&rsquo;t be joined in this way by only the comma before \"a type.\" Choice D is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Using the finite present tense verb \"is characterized\" creates a second main clause in the sentence, and the two main clauses can&rsquo;t be joined in this way by only the comma before \"a type.\"</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669213,"updateDate":1743430669213}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669213, 1743430669213),
    ($SATQ$f40ca576$SATQ$, $SATQ$5b1c2438-3440-4e43-8c57-b478c7ed0be7$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$7ee23969-0c6a-4e08-934a-1b5738675862$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Each year in the fall, when the weather starts to cool in the northern hemisphere, millions of North American monarch butterflies journey south. Searching for food and warmer habitats, they <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> thousands of miles&mdash;from as far north as Canada all the way down to Mexico&mdash;on this annual migration.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"190c9e3b-0430-40c8-9c18-b18b8fb5f69f","content":"<p>flew</p>"},{"id":"26d797aa-a815-41ce-b56c-267bd0c1daad","content":"<p>were flying</p>"},{"id":"d007b47b-3443-404a-b78b-c4f9f273827e","content":"<p>had flown</p>"},{"id":"68306de2-02bb-481c-82d4-bc99319ff107","content":"<p>fly</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["68306de2-02bb-481c-82d4-bc99319ff107"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of verbs to express tense. In this choice, the present tense verb &ldquo;fly&rdquo; is consistent with the other present tense verb ( &ldquo;journey&rdquo;) used to describe the butterflies&rsquo; yearly migration. Together, these simple present tense verbs correctly indicate that the migration is a current, yearly occurrence. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The simple past tense verb &ldquo;flew&rdquo; isn&rsquo;t consistent with the other present tense verb used to describe the butterflies&rsquo; yearly migration. Choice B is incorrect. The past progressive tense verb &ldquo;were flying&rdquo; isn&rsquo;t consistent with the other present tense verb used to describe the butterflies&rsquo; yearly migration. Choice C is incorrect. The past perfect tense verb &ldquo;had flown&rdquo; isn&rsquo;t consistent with the other present tense verb used to describe the butterflies&rsquo; yearly migration. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-736-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$d6e07fc1-fb62-4e25-a5fb-ce3aec56e0b7$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"f40ca576","external_id":"5b1c2438-3440-4e43-8c57-b478c7ed0be7","vaultid":"7ee23969-0c6a-4e08-934a-1b5738675862","uId":"bdbdf7ed-7b6e-4f44-81ab-06e15a1822a1","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Each year in the fall, when the weather starts to cool in the northern hemisphere, millions of North American monarch butterflies journey south. Searching for food and warmer habitats, they <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> thousands of miles&mdash;from as far north as Canada all the way down to Mexico&mdash;on this annual migration.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"190c9e3b-0430-40c8-9c18-b18b8fb5f69f","content":"<p>flew</p>"},{"id":"26d797aa-a815-41ce-b56c-267bd0c1daad","content":"<p>were flying</p>"},{"id":"d007b47b-3443-404a-b78b-c4f9f273827e","content":"<p>had flown</p>"},{"id":"68306de2-02bb-481c-82d4-bc99319ff107","content":"<p>fly</p>"}],"keys":["68306de2-02bb-481c-82d4-bc99319ff107"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of verbs to express tense. In this choice, the present tense verb &ldquo;fly&rdquo; is consistent with the other present tense verb ( &ldquo;journey&rdquo;) used to describe the butterflies&rsquo; yearly migration. Together, these simple present tense verbs correctly indicate that the migration is a current, yearly occurrence. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The simple past tense verb &ldquo;flew&rdquo; isn&rsquo;t consistent with the other present tense verb used to describe the butterflies&rsquo; yearly migration. Choice B is incorrect. The past progressive tense verb &ldquo;were flying&rdquo; isn&rsquo;t consistent with the other present tense verb used to describe the butterflies&rsquo; yearly migration. Choice C is incorrect. The past perfect tense verb &ldquo;had flown&rdquo; isn&rsquo;t consistent with the other present tense verb used to describe the butterflies&rsquo; yearly migration. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-736-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"d6e07fc1-fb62-4e25-a5fb-ce3aec56e0b7","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959349,"updateDate":1691007959349}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959349, 1691007959349),
    ($SATQ$f45ae404$SATQ$, $SATQ$859b0c99-ba39-402b-950e-6ea7f8512193$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The city of Amsterdam partnered with consultants to develop Public Eye&mdash;an ethical AI-powered crowd-monitoring <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> video streamed from cameras in heavily touristed areas, the AI algorithm determines crowd sizes without, in the interest of protecting individuals&rsquo; privacy, retaining the footage.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"4dc4f9ef-f159-49f4-b34e-3338b8fb6179","content":"<p>&nbsp;system&mdash;analyzing</p>"},{"id":"143fc10b-e5ac-4806-a608-1d5a3a72ba57","content":"<p>system, analyzing</p>"},{"id":"f35a75f4-62ce-46f1-b19c-71f9d5bc1e09","content":"<p>system. Analyzing</p>"},{"id":"aafbb0d4-a727-4615-a128-5376c0bac7a5","content":"<p>system analyzing</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["f35a75f4-62ce-46f1-b19c-71f9d5bc1e09"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation use between sentences. In this choice, the period is used correctly to mark the boundary between one sentence ("The city&hellip;system") and another ("Analyzing&hellip;footage"). The participial phrase beginning with "analyzing" modifies the subject of the second sentence, "the AI algorithm."</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Placing a dash before "analyzing" creates a confusing and ambiguous modifying element ("analyzing...areas") and a comma splice between "areas" and "the AI algorithm." (A comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between sentences.) Choice B is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. A comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between sentences. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The sentences ("The city&hellip;system" and "analyzing&hellip;footage") are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"f45ae404","external_id":"859b0c99-ba39-402b-950e-6ea7f8512193","vaultid":null,"uId":"b7fe1e29-8f43-4749-be62-42d1bb0e28c3","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The city of Amsterdam partnered with consultants to develop Public Eye&mdash;an ethical AI-powered crowd-monitoring <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> video streamed from cameras in heavily touristed areas, the AI algorithm determines crowd sizes without, in the interest of protecting individuals&rsquo; privacy, retaining the footage.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"4dc4f9ef-f159-49f4-b34e-3338b8fb6179","content":"<p>&nbsp;system&mdash;analyzing</p>"},{"id":"143fc10b-e5ac-4806-a608-1d5a3a72ba57","content":"<p>system, analyzing</p>"},{"id":"f35a75f4-62ce-46f1-b19c-71f9d5bc1e09","content":"<p>system. Analyzing</p>"},{"id":"aafbb0d4-a727-4615-a128-5376c0bac7a5","content":"<p>system analyzing</p>"}],"keys":["f35a75f4-62ce-46f1-b19c-71f9d5bc1e09"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation use between sentences. In this choice, the period is used correctly to mark the boundary between one sentence (\"The city&hellip;system\") and another (\"Analyzing&hellip;footage\"). The participial phrase beginning with \"analyzing\" modifies the subject of the second sentence, \"the AI a